---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';
import wineries from '../../data/wineries.json';

const kidFriendly = [...wineries]
  .filter((w) => w.kid_friendly?.allowed)
  .sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0))
  .slice(0, 10);

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Temecula Wine Tasting with Kids — Practical Family Guide',
  datePublished: '2026-09-08',
  dateModified: '2026-09-08',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bellie Acres Wine' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bellie Acres Wine', url: 'https://bellieacreswine.com/' },
  description:
    'Temecula wine tasting with kids: which estates allow children, pacing, food, nap windows, and how to keep the day fun without pretending a tasting room is a playground.',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://bellieacreswine.com/guides/temecula-wine-tasting-with-kids/',
};
---

<Layout
  title="Temecula Wine Tasting with Kids | Family Wine Country Guide"
  description="Bring kids to Temecula wine country without chaos: kid-friendly estate tips, stop counts, food, timing, and honest limits. Independent Bellie Acres guide."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Navbar />
  <main class="bg-[#faf7f2]">
    <header class="border-b border-stone-200 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
        <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[2px] text-[var(--accent)] font-medium mb-3">Guide · Families · Sep 2026</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-serif tracking-tight text-stone-900 leading-[1.1]">
          Temecula Wine Tasting with Kids
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg sm:text-xl text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
          Yes, you can taste wine in Temecula with children in the party — if you pick the right estates, cut the stop
          count, and plan like a parent, not like a bachelor weekend. This guide is practical: policies, pacing, food,
          heat, and the limits nobody puts on the brochure.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-stone-500">
          Independent guide from <a href="/about" class="underline hover:text-stone-800">Bellie Acres Wine</a>. Kid
          policies change — always confirm with the winery before you go. Alcohol tastings are 21+.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-12 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">The honest short answer</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Some Temecula estates welcome kids; some do not; many allow children outdoors or in specific areas
          only.</strong> A tasting room is still an adult alcohol environment. Your job is to choose places where a
          restless kid is manageable, keep the day short, feed everyone, and never treat the patio like a free babysitter.
        </p>
        <p>
          Default to <strong>two stops</strong> (maybe a soft third if everyone is thriving). Read
          <a href="/guides/how-many-wineries-one-day" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">how many wineries in one day</a>
          and then subtract one for childhood. Use the
          <a href="/wineries" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">wineries list</a> filters and call ahead — directory
          tags help; official policy wins.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-3xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-serif text-stone-900">What “kid-friendly” usually means</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Children allowed on grounds or patio, not necessarily at the bar rail during a seated flight</li>
          <li>Space to move, lawns, or outdoor seating where a stroller fits</li>
          <li>Food options that are not only wine-and-cheese for adults</li>
          <li>Staff who have seen a toddler before and will not panic at crayon volume</li>
          <li>Clear rules: no running near pour stations, no kids tasting alcohol, no unsupervised exploration of production areas</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          It does <em>not</em> mean unlimited free childcare, splash pads at every address, or that every adult guest
          wanted a family reunion next to their Cabernet flight. Be the group that makes kid-friendly policies sustainable.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Build the day around the smallest human</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Nap and meal windows rule the calendar</strong> — not the influencer itinerary. If your child melts at
          2 p.m., do not schedule the “best” tasting at 1:45. Morning seatings often win: cooler, calmer, shorter lines.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>One parent fully on kid duty</strong> while the other tastes — or swap halves of the flight. Trying to
          dual-wield a glass and a sprinting preschooler is how wine ends up on linen and patience ends up in the car.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Bring the boring gear:</strong> snacks you know they will eat, water, wipes, sunscreen, hat, a quiet
          activity, change of clothes. Do not rely on gift-shop crackers as a complete nutrition plan.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Designate a sober driver</strong> who is truly sober. Kids in the car raise the stakes; rideshare with
          car seats is logistics-heavy — plan it before the first pour.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Stop count and corridor choice</h2>
        <p>
          Main-road destination estates with food and lawns can be easier than tiny quiet rooms with no buffer space —
          but weekends on
          <a href="/guides/rancho-california-road-wine-tasting" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">Rancho California Road</a>
          are louder. Quieter
          <a href="/guides/de-portola-road-wine-tasting" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">De Portola</a>
          days can be lovely if the estate explicitly allows kids and you keep expectations modest.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two clustered stops beat three heroic ones. If the first stop is a win, leave while joy is high. Ending on a
          tantrum in a third parking lot teaches nobody about Syrah.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Estates with kid-friendly tags in our directory</h2>
        <p>
          These listings carry a kid-friendly flag in our data. <strong>Call or check the winery site the week you go</strong>
          — events, private buyouts, and policy updates override any directory.
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          {kidFriendly.map((w) => (
            <li class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white px-4 py-3">
              <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`} class="font-medium text-stone-900 hover:text-[var(--accent)]">
                {w.name}
              </a>
              <span class="text-stone-500 text-sm">
                {' '}
                · {w.price_range} · {w.rating}/5 · {(w as any).corridor || w.location}
              </span>
              {w.kid_friendly?.notes && (
                <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mt-1">{w.kid_friendly.notes}</p>
              )}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p>
          Full searchable list:
          <a href="/wineries" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">Temecula wineries directory</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Food, shade, and heat</h2>
        <p>
          Hungry kids and warm wine-country afternoons are a bad cocktail. Prioritize estates with real food or plan a
          proper lunch break off-site. Shade matters — strollers on full-sun gravel turn into ovens. Summer strategy
          overlaps our heat guidance: earlier tastings, water always in the car, shorter final stop.
        </p>
        <p>
          Non-alcoholic options for the designated adult and older teens (where allowed on property — still no underage
          alcohol) keep the table from feeling like a punishment zone. Ask about juice, soda, or mocktails without
          embarrassment.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Etiquette that keeps doors open</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Reserve and mention kids if the form allows notes — surprises help nobody</li>
          <li>Keep volume reasonable; step outside for meltdowns</li>
          <li>Do not let children handle glasses, spit cups, or production equipment</li>
          <li>Tip well when staff help your family succeed</li>
          <li>Skip peak festival stomps unless the event is explicitly family-designed</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Harvest weekends are magical and crowded — see
          <a href="/guides/temecula-harvest-season" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">harvest season</a>
          before you bring littles into peak chaos.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">When to leave the kids with a sitter</h2>
        <p>
          Choose adult-only if you want long seated pairings, heavy weekend party energy, multiple serious red flights,
          or a proposal/anniversary mood that needs quiet. There is no moral failure in hiring help and doing a deeper
          tasting day. Temecula will still be there next season — with or without crayons.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Sample family day (template)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>10:00</strong> — Arrive first kid-friendly estate; order water immediately; one adult starts the flight
          while the other walks the lawn edge.  
          <strong>11:15</strong> — Snack in the car; bathroom break.  
          <strong>11:45</strong> — Short drive to stop two (already clustered on the
          <a href="/map" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">map</a>).  
          <strong>12:00–1:15</strong> — Second tasting or shared food-focused visit.  
          <strong>1:30</strong> — Real lunch or head home before the heat-and-nap crash.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is a full win. Resist the “one more famous place” add-on unless both kids and adults still have margin.
          Weekday versions of this template are calmer; weekend versions need holds — see
          <a href="/guides/weekday-vs-weekend-tasting" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">weekday vs weekend</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If grandparents are along, assign roles explicitly: who tastes, who spots the toddler near the parking curb,
          who holds the reservation QR. Ambiguity creates the only kind of blend you do not want.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Bottom line</h2>
        <p>
          Wine tasting with kids in Temecula works when policy, pacing, food, and parental attention are designed on
          purpose. Two good stops beat four miserable ones. Confirm kid rules, cluster on the map, keep someone sober,
          and exit while the day is still a win.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next:
          <a href="/guides/weekday-vs-weekend-tasting" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">weekday vs weekend</a>,
          <a href="/guides/pet-friendly-wineries" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">pets</a> (different logistics),
          <a href="/wine-day-planner/" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">free Day Planner PDF</a>,
          <a href="/guides" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">all guides</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
